{"id": "fresh-0", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 3.056590223459901, "ttft_seconds": 11.875896412995644, "text": "# Database Transaction Recovery After Power Failure\n\nA power failure is the canonical crash scenario that every transactional database must survive. The guarantee that matters is **durability**: once a transaction commits, its effects must persist even if the machine loses power one microsecond later. The complementary guarantee is **atomicity**: a transaction that was interrupted mid-flight must leave no partial trace. This article explains the mechanisms databases use to deliver both, walks through concrete worked examples, and discusses where the guarantees break down.\n\n## The Core Problem\n\nA database keeps its data on disk, but disk writes are slow relative to CPU and memory. To achieve reasonable throughput, databases"}
{"id": "fresh-1", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 3.635412156828798, "ttft_seconds": 12.266207812004723, "text": "# How a Compiler Detects and Optimizes Repeated Expressions\n\nRepeated expressions\u2014computing the same value more than once\u2014are among the most common sources of wasted work in programs. Compilers attack this problem with a family of techniques collectively called **redundancy elimination**, of which **common subexpression elimination (CSE)** is the canonical member. This article explains how a compiler *detects* that two expressions compute the same value, how it *rewrites* the program to compute that value once, and where these techniques break down.\n\n## 1. The Core Idea: Value Numbering\n\nThe central abstraction behind"}
{"id": "fresh-2", "phase": "fresh", "decode_tok_s": 4.02332789124287, "ttft_seconds": 11.403719100999297, "text": "# TCP Congestion Control: Adaptation to Changing Bandwidth\n\nTCP congestion control is the set of algorithms that governs how aggressively a sender injects packets into the network. Its central challenge is that the network's available bandwidth is unknown, time-varying, and shared with other flows. TCP must therefore *discover* capacity empirically\u2014probing upward until loss or delay signals congestion, then backing off\u2014while remaining fair to competing flows and efficient enough to use the link. This essay explains the mechanisms by which TCP adapts to changing bandwidth, works through concrete numerical examples, and discusses the limitations of the approach.\n\n## The Core Feedback Loop\n\nClass"}
